# {{NAME}}

A connector is a skill directory plus one script (ADR 37480 D1). This one was
scaffolded by the `connector-author` skill from the daemon's starter template
on {{DATE}}; the interview answers it was built from are in
`tests/answers.json`. `<connector-script>` is `scripts/{{SCRIPT_NAME}}`
joined against this skill's `skill-dir` (the wrapper that delivered this body
carries it; never the `path` attribute).

## What it connects

- **Source:** {{SOURCE_NAME}} — {{SOURCE_SUMMARY}} The `--source` word is
  `{{SOURCE_WORD}}`.
- **Delivery:** {{DELIVERY_SENTENCE}}
- **One conversation is** {{CONVERSATION_UNIT}}; its stable id is
  {{CONVERSATION_ID_RULE}}. The alias `c<n>` is this connector's own counter
  (another connector's `c1` is a different conversation; the daemon
  addresses `(connector, alias)`).
- **Identity and auth:** {{AUTH_IDENTITY}} The lane environment names are
  {{AUTH_ENV_LIST}} (`status --json` reports them as `env_pass`; never a
  value in a thread).
- **Default filter:** `{{DEFAULT_FILTER}}` — {{FILTER_MEANING}} A wider
  stream is asked for in the connect prompt (`--filter ''` is everything);
  two live subscriptions on one source need disjoint filters.

## The three rules this connector keeps (ADR 37480 D1, D8)

1. Ownership and lanes are the daemon's: the script mints an opaque
   `conversation` id per {{CONVERSATION_UNIT}} and never reads the registry.
2. `listen` prints one line per inbound message, `c<n> <sender>: <text>`,
   and nothing else on stdout (its own notices go to stderr).
3. A second `listen` for a live subscription key (`source`, `filter`) is
   refused with one JSON line (`listener_conflict`, exit 3; an overlapping
   filter on the same source is `filter_overlap`) naming the live pid; `reply`
   is idempotent on `--key` (a repeat returns the first receipt with
   `"idempotent": true`).

## Commands

```
monitor(command="python3 <connector-script> listen --source {{SOURCE_WORD}} [--filter <word>]", persistent=true, wake_delay_ms=0, show_lines=true, description="{{NAME}}")
python3 <connector-script> reply --to c<n> --text <text> [--key <k>] [--origin <connector>/<conversation>]
python3 <connector-script> describe --to c<n>
python3 <connector-script> status --json
python3 <connector-script> disconnect
```

`status --json` is the contract the daemon's `start` reads on every restart:
`sources_available` (every `--source` word `listen` honours; a recorded row
whose word is missing is reported `stale`, never armed) and `listeners` with
one record per source word (`listener`, `pid`, `subscriptions`) beside one per
live subscription key (`<source>|<filter>`). A record whose last listener
ended non-zero carries `exit` (integer) and `ended_at` (ISO time) until a
new listener starts on it: `start` records that failure itself, without a
model call. A live listener, a clean end and a stop by signal carry neither.

Connect words for the daemon prompt: {{CONNECT_WORDS}} → `/daemon connect
{{NAME}}` (or `muse daemon connect {{NAME}}`). The daemon records one intent
row for this connector (`start --connector {{NAME}} --connector-script
<connector-script> --source {{SOURCE_WORD}} --filter {{DEFAULT_FILTER}}
--prompt '<the words>'`) and arms the Monitor above.

## Replies

{{REPLY_SENTENCE}} Plain text{{MARKDOWN_NOTE}}; no cards, attachments or
edits (`status --json` says so). Never @-mention your own handle in a reply:
it would come back as a new line.

